WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.679 --> 00:00:06.819
Cats might have nine lives but the film adaptation
of Cats was basically dead on arrival.

00:00:06.819 --> 00:00:11.590
Perhaps it sounds overly dramatic to declare
Tom Hooper's Cats a full-fledged cinematic

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:14.890
calamity… but at this point, the facts are
rather indisputable.

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:21.320
Now, after a few weeks in theaters, the film
stands to lose a whopping $100 million for

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:22.730
Universal.

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:27.260
That's even after its unprecedented theatrical
re-release, which found the filmmakers attempting

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:30.949
to "fix" the terrifying and ever-offputting
CGI felines.

00:00:30.949 --> 00:00:36.200
"Seems like they scrambled and they rushed
to re-edit the film."

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:42.370
Meanwhile, the studio quietly yanked Cats
from its "For Your Consideration" page, effectively

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:46.160
ending the film's Oscar campaign before it
had a chance to really begin.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.219
Don't make us say it: The film is one serious
cat-astrophe.

00:00:50.219 --> 00:00:54.610
“Bah dum tiss”

00:00:54.610 --> 00:01:00.220
Between its abysmal box office performance
and wildly low Rotten Tomatoes score, it's

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:05.610
clear that audiences and critics really didn't
care for Cats... and just looking at the trailers,

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:07.210
it's not too hard to see why.

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:12.340
So what went wrong with this Broadway-sensation-turned-box-office
stinker?

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:17.381
Well, in a recent Reddit thread, several VFX
technicians who allegedly worked on the film

00:01:17.381 --> 00:01:22.440
reveal what went down behind the scenes…
and the situation, if true, sounds pretty

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:23.440
dire.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:28.570
Apparently, we can place the blame squarely
on impossible deadlines, unrealistic demands,

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:31.700
and numerous poor decisions made by Hooper.

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:36.130
"We've used digital fur technology to create
the most perfect covering of fur."

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:38.540
"These are people… but they're cats!

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:40.980
And this is kind-of blowing my mind."

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:45.490
As we mentioned, Universal pulled a stunning
move shortly after the film hit theaters,

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:50.270
announcing that Cats had to be re-released
in order to address some very obvious CGI

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:51.270
blunders.

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:55.810
For one, there was the generally unsettling
look of the human faces haphazardly pasted

00:01:55.810 --> 00:02:01.240
onto digital cat bodies... not to mention
a major muck-up involving Dame Judi Dench's

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:04.119
cat character, Old Deuteronomy.

00:02:04.119 --> 00:02:09.149
That particular mistake was publicized by
Variety's Jenelle Riley, who wrote on Twitter:

00:02:09.149 --> 00:02:14.709
"This isn't a joke: CATS was rushed into theaters
before being finished so a new version is

00:02:14.709 --> 00:02:17.750
being sent to theaters with updated effects.

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:20.650
How do you know if you have the old version?

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:25.809
Look for Judi Dench's human hand, wedding
ring and all."

00:02:25.809 --> 00:02:31.489
Also worth noting: The production schedule
was reportedly so rushed, Hooper kept maniacally

00:02:31.489 --> 00:02:36.329
working on what was meant to be the final
cut of the film… right up to Cats' premiere.

00:02:36.329 --> 00:02:38.150
On the red carpet, he told Variety,

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:42.269
"I finished it at 8 a.m. yesterday after 36
hours in a row.

00:02:42.269 --> 00:02:44.540
I just put the finishing touches on.

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:47.569
So, I’m very happy to be here with it fully
finished.

00:02:47.569 --> 00:02:52.840
The premiere should be the first time people
get to see it, and this is genuinely a premiere."

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:58.290
Meanwhile, We Got This Covered sums up the
Reddit thread by noting that, quote, "poor

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:03.589
requests from the studio and ridiculously
short deadlines made quality work near-impossible."

00:03:03.589 --> 00:03:07.319
In fact, someone claiming to have worked on
the film's visual effects wrote,

00:03:07.319 --> 00:03:12.099
"Blame the director and clients for their
lack of realistic requests."

00:03:12.099 --> 00:03:15.019
According to another supposed VFX technician:

00:03:15.019 --> 00:03:19.839
"Often cats were missing layers, lights, and
cryptos were unusably broken.

00:03:19.839 --> 00:03:24.189
But you couldn't ask for a rerender unless
there was something egregious like a whole

00:03:24.189 --> 00:03:25.559
cat missing.

00:03:25.559 --> 00:03:30.559
The show was one uphill battle for every artist
and [supervisor] involved, with a client that

00:03:30.559 --> 00:03:35.349
could only identify what they [didn't] like,
and [who wouldn't] offer any clues as to how

00:03:35.349 --> 00:03:37.059
to get to what they did like.

00:03:37.059 --> 00:03:39.029
We did what we could."

00:03:39.029 --> 00:03:40.099
Another Redditor added:

00:03:40.099 --> 00:03:43.949
"The timelines of these productions is the
thing that's frustrating.

00:03:43.949 --> 00:03:48.579
This movie still may have been a flop even
if it [had been] done well, but the super

00:03:48.579 --> 00:03:50.930
short schedule is what killed this movie."

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:55.790
Well, as you can see for yourself, the end
result looks incredibly bizarre.

00:03:55.790 --> 00:04:00.180
Perhaps one of the biggest problems with Cats
is that the movie feels rather driftless and

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:01.359
purposeless.

00:04:01.359 --> 00:04:05.659
Adapted into a stage play by Andrew Lloyd
Webber from a book of poetry by T.S.

00:04:05.659 --> 00:04:10.939
Eliot called Old Possum's Book of Practical
Cats, the musical follows the adventures of

00:04:10.939 --> 00:04:13.090
a group of London street cats.

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:17.989
Each of them yearns to ascend to the so-called
"Heaviside Layer" in order to be reborn.

00:04:17.989 --> 00:04:22.099
At the end of the day, it turns out that a
bunch of singing cats fighting over who gets

00:04:22.099 --> 00:04:26.300
to die isn't what box-office bonanzas are
made of.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:30.280
Audience members who did wind up watching
the film likely left the theater wondering

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:31.930
what the hell they'd just seen.

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:36.580
We have the answer for that one: They'd just
seen one of the biggest box-office bombs in

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:38.490
recent memory.

